      {
        "id": "p48-q11-c-node",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Show that the function is even. What symmetry does its graph exhibit?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f(-x)=f(x)$; line symmetry about the $y$-axis.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(-x)&=\\frac{1}{(-x)^2-4}\\\\f(-x)&=\\frac{1}{x^2-4}\\\\f(-x)&=f(x).\\end{align*}$$\nThus $f$ is even and its graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p48-q11-d-node",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "State the domain of the function and the equations of any vertical asymptotes.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Domain: $\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{-2,2\\}$. Vertical asymptotes: $x=-2$ and $x=2$.",
          "worked": "The denominator is zero when $x^2-4=0$, giving $x=\\pm2$. These values are excluded from the domain. The numerator is nonzero there, so $x=-2$ and $x=2$ are vertical asymptotes.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p48-q11-e-node",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "What value does $f(x)$ approach as $x\\to\\infty$ and as $x\\to-\\infty$?\nHence write down the equation of the horizontal asymptote.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f(x)\\to0$ in both directions. Horizontal asymptote: $y=0$.",
          "worked": "As $x\\to\\pm\\infty$, $x^2-4\\to\\infty$, so $\\dfrac{1}{x^2-4}\\to0$ from above. Hence the horizontal asymptote is $y=0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 10
      },
      {
        "id": "p48-q11-f-node",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "Sketch the graph of $y=f(x)$, showing all important features.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Symmetric about the $y$-axis, with vertical asymptotes $x=\\pm2$, horizontal asymptote $y=0$, local maximum and $y$-intercept $(0,-\\tfrac14)$, and no $x$-intercepts.",
          "worked": "There are no $x$-intercepts because the numerator is never zero. The outer branches are positive, approach $y=0$ as $x\\to\\pm\\infty$ and approach $+\\infty$ beside the vertical asymptotes. The central branch is negative, has a local maximum at $(0,-\\tfrac14)$ and approaches $-\\infty$ as $x\\to-2^+$ or $x\\to2^-$. Reflect either half in the $y$-axis.",
          "solutionDiagrams": [
